# Resolution
Stale on current `main`.

The finding was valid for commit `5e7f1f0`, where `run_remote_frontend`
constructed the frontend coordinator with background workers disabled. Current
code no longer matches that premise:

- `1595c5e` (`Enable remote shard scavenger worker mode`) introduced explicit
  remote frontend background-worker mode wiring and focused coverage for the
  mode.
- `ec10f04` (`Enable remote reclaim workers`) enabled remote
  object-payload reclaim and bucket-delete finalization workers after routing
  their metadata surfaces over RPC.
- `1e69c32` (`Enable remote lifecycle worker`) enabled remote lifecycle worker
  operation after routing lifecycle root/claim handling through RPC.
- `8de4825` (`Route stream session scavenging over storage RPC`) moved stale
  stream-upload session scavenging to a bounded PG-wide RPC and removed the
  remaining broad local scan from remote frontend worker scope.

Current references:
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:267` calls `run_frontend_server` with
  `BackgroundWorkerMode::remote_frontend_phase_10_6()` for remote frontend and
  combined roles.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/infra.rs:45` defines
  `remote_frontend_phase_10_6()` with object reclaim/bucket finalization,
  lifecycle, and shard scavenger all enabled.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/infra.rs:343` uses that mode to spawn or
  acquire the corresponding background workers.
- `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/core_tests.rs:89` asserts the remote
  frontend Phase 10.6 mode enables all routed workers.
- `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs` contains focused Unix-storage-node
  regressions for remote reclaim/finalization, lifecycle, shard-scavenger
  metadata, and stale stream-session scavenging against storage-node-owned PGs
  rather than frontend placeholder PGs.

Residual note: the full separate-process frontend/storage-node harness is still
Phase 10.7 work, and frontend-only topology still opens transitional placeholder
PG directories until that harness/topology phase. Those are not the same issue
as the stale finding here: remote frontend no longer leaves reclaim,
finalization, lifecycle, or shard-scavenger background work disabled.

# Summary
Introduced: remote frontend and combined roles are now runnable, but their coordinator is explicitly constructed with reclaim/lifecycle/scavenger background work disabled. This leaves queued reclaim work undrained for those roles.
This commit enables the previously unsupported remote frontend roles. In that path, run_remote_frontend passes start_background_sweepers=false into run_frontend_server. That selects the new Coordinator constructor named without_background_sweepers, which passes false for the reclaim worker and disabled lifecycle and shard-scavenger sweepers. The reclaim worker is the code that consumes ReclaimWorkItem::ObjectPayload and ReclaimWorkItem::BucketDelete and calls try_reclaim_object_payload_for / try_finalize_bucket_delete_for. With it disabled and no replacement worker started in the storage-node process, remote frontend deployments can accept S3 deletes, overwrites, multipart aborts, and bucket deletes that update metadata but never physically reclaim the old shard data or finalize queued bucket deletion work. An authenticated client can repeatedly upload and delete or overwrite objects to drive unbounded backend disk growth while the visible object set remains small, eventually causing storage exhaustion and denial of service. Combined role is also affected because it starts the same remote frontend path.

