# Attack-path analysis
Final: medium | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: medium | Policy adjusted: medium
## Rationale
Kept at medium. The bug is real and in-scope: product runtime code enables Frontend/Combined roles and intentionally constructs their coordinator without background sweepers, while normal S3 delete/overwrite/bucket-delete paths enqueue reclaim work that only the disabled worker normally processes. The impact is a credible availability DoS via disk exhaustion, and validation evidence included an executable PoC demonstrating undrained reclaim work after an authenticated delete using the same no-background-sweeper constructor. However, exploitation requires a remote frontend/combined deployment, a reachable listener, and valid S3 write/delete privileges; repository artifacts show the default listener is localhost and the default role is not the affected remote frontend path. There is no confidentiality, integrity, auth bypass, or RCE impact, so high/critical is not justified.
## Likelihood
high - Once a remote frontend/combined deployment is reachable and the attacker has ordinary S3 write/delete credentials, exploitation is simple and repeatable using normal S3 operations. Likelihood is not high because the default process role is legacy-local, the default listener is localhost, and exploitation requires valid credentials and a specific newly enabled role. | Remote network vector
## Impact
medium - Successful exploitation can exhaust backend storage and deny service to the argmin-s3 deployment. The visible object set can remain small because metadata deletes/overwrites succeed while physical shard payloads are not reclaimed. No code execution, credential disclosure, authorization bypass, or object data exfiltration is evidenced.
## Assumptions
- Deployment selects ARGMIN_PROCESS_ROLE=frontend or ARGMIN_PROCESS_ROLE=combined, not the default legacy-local role.
- ARGMIN_STORAGE_NODE_SOCKETS is configured so the remote frontend role starts successfully.
- The S3 HTTP listener is reachable by the attacker, either locally/internal or because ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR is bound or proxied to a reachable interface.
- The attacker has valid S3 credentials with permission to upload/delete/overwrite objects or delete buckets in at least one bucket.
- No external quota, disk cleanup job, or operational monitoring stops repeated upload/delete cycles before backend storage exhaustion.
- Remote frontend or combined role enabled
- Storage-node socket map configured
- Reachable S3 HTTP endpoint
- Authenticated S3 write/delete capability
- Finite backend disk without compensating external cleanup/quota
## Path
Authenticated S3 client
  -> HTTP Put/Delete/Overwrite
  -> Frontend/Combined run_remote_frontend
  -> run_frontend_server(..., false)
  -> Coordinator without ReclaimSweeper
  -> reclaim queue accumulates
  -> backend disk exhaustion / DoS
